# Technology Stack

## Confirmed Choices

| Layer | Technology | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Framework | **Next.js 15** (App Router) | React-based, SSR via Server Components, deployed on Vercel |
| Repo Structure | **Single repo, single package** | No monorepo, no Turborepo, no workspaces. One package.json. |
| Deployment | **Vercel** | Zero-config Next.js deployment, edge functions, preview deploys |
| Styling | **Tailwind CSS v4** | Utility-first, custom brew/vegas theme |
| Component Primitives | **Radix UI** | Accessible headless components (modals, dropdowns, tooltips) |
| Data Fetching (client) | **TanStack Query** | Client-side caching, mutations, optimistic updates |
| Data Fetching (server) | **Next.js Server Components** | SSR data loading, no client-side spinner for initial loads |
| Tables | **TanStack Table** | Sorting, filtering, pagination for stat tables |
| Class Utilities | **clsx + tailwind-merge** | Conditional Tailwind class composition |
| Icons | **Lucide React** | Clean, consistent icon set |
| Fonts | **next/font** | Self-hosted Inter, JetBrains Mono — no layout shift |
| Testing | **Vitest + React Testing Library** | TDD workflow (see tdd-workflow.mdc) |
| Visual Testing | **Storybook** | Component catalog, visual validation (see storybook.mdc) |
| Linting | **ESLint + eslint-plugin-boundaries** | Enforces import boundary rules |
| Authentication | **AWS Cognito (Hosted UI) + BFF Token Relay** | See Authentication section below |
| Session Management | **iron-session** (or equivalent) | Encrypted HTTP-only cookies, server-side only |
| Package Manager | **npm** | |

## Authentication Architecture

### Pattern: BFF Token Relay with Cognito Hosted UI

Authentication uses **Authorization Code Flow with PKCE** via the Cognito Hosted UI. The BFF (Next.js Route Handlers) manages the entire token lifecycle. **Tokens never reach the browser.**

### Flow

1. User clicks "Sign In" → BFF redirects browser to Cognito Hosted UI with PKCE challenge
2. User authenticates on Cognito's form → Cognito redirects to `/api/auth/callback` with authorization code
3. BFF exchanges the authorization code + PKCE verifier for tokens (access, ID, refresh) **server-side**
4. BFF encrypts tokens into an HTTP-only, Secure, SameSite=Strict session cookie
5. Browser makes API calls to BFF routes → BFF reads session cookie, extracts access token, attaches as `Authorization: Bearer` header to AWS API calls
6. When the access token expires, BFF uses the refresh token to get a new one — transparent to the browser
7. AWS backend validates the JWT (signature, expiry, issuer, audience) — standard OAuth 2.0

### Security Properties

- **Tokens never exist in client-side JavaScript** — no XSS risk for token theft
- **Browser only has an encrypted HTTP-only cookie** — JS cannot read it
- **Refresh tokens are server-side only** — never exposed to the client
- **PKCE** prevents authorization code interception attacks
- **SameSite=Strict cookie** mitigates CSRF

### OAuth 2.0 Grant Details

- **Grant type:** Authorization Code with PKCE
- **Scopes:** `openid profile email` (minimum; extend as needed)
- **Token types:** Access token (JWT, sent to AWS APIs), ID token (JWT, user profile info), Refresh token (opaque, stored server-side)
- **Token validation:** AWS backend validates access token JWTs against Cognito's JWKS endpoint at `https://cognito-idp.{region}.amazonaws.com/{userPoolId}/.well-known/jwks.json`

### What This Repo Owns (Frontend)

| Route | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `/api/auth/login` | Generates PKCE verifier/challenge, builds Cognito authorization URL, redirects browser |
| `/api/auth/callback` | Receives auth code, exchanges for tokens server-side, sets encrypted session cookie, redirects to app |
| `/api/auth/logout` | Clears session cookie, redirects to Cognito logout endpoint |
| `/api/auth/session` | Returns current user profile (from ID token) for client components |
| `middleware.ts` | Protects app routes — redirects to login if no valid session cookie |
| `src/features/auth/` | `useSession` hook for client components to read auth state |

### What the AWS Backend Owns

| Layer | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| Cognito User Pool | User management, passwords, MFA, hosted sign-in UI, token issuance |
| Cognito App Client | Configured with Authorization Code + PKCE, callback URL set to Vercel domain |
| API Gateway | Cognito authorizer (or custom Lambda authorizer) validates JWT on every request |
| Lambda / services | Receives pre-validated requests, reads user claims (sub, email, groups) from decoded token |

## Backend

- Backend APIs are in a **separate AWS codebase** (not this repo).
- This frontend communicates with AWS via the BFF layer (`src/app/api/`).
- AWS services, database, and business logic are not a concern of this codebase.
- The BFF attaches the user's Cognito access token (JWT) to all API calls via `Authorization: Bearer` header.
